                            {
                              "scene_number": 2,
                              "scene_title": "The Scholar's Bargain",
                              "setup": "While exploring an ancient library complex in Pangaea, Francisco becomes separated from the Catalan Company. He encounters Dagon Atumari, who appears as a distinguished scholar with vast knowledge. Dagon recognizes Francisco's intellectual hunger and begins their conversation by discussing Cicero's lost works, particularly 'On Glory.' He reveals intimate knowledge of Francisco's academic struggles and dreams, making Francisco feel understood in a way he's never experienced.",
                              "symbolism": "The library represents Francisco's intellectual pride and desire for knowledge. Dagon's scholarly appearance masks his true nature, symbolizing how wisdom can be corrupted. The separation from his companions represents Francisco's isolation when faced with temptation.",
                              "beat_goal": "Introduce Dagon as a compelling tempter who appeals directly to Francisco's greatest weakness - his intellectual vanity. Establish their dynamic as student and master, setting up Francisco's vulnerability to manipulation.",
