                            {
                              "scene_number": 3,
                              "scene_title": "The Crown's Promise",
                              "setup": "Dagon reveals the existence of the Crown of the Ancient Ones, claiming it will grant Francisco the knowledge and power to not only find Cicero's lost works but to understand the deepest mysteries of existence. He presents this as a scholarly opportunity rather than a quest for power, appealing to Francisco's academic nature. Francisco, overwhelmed by the possibility of such knowledge and feeling intellectually superior to his companions who 'wouldn't understand,' agrees to retrieve the Crown in exchange for 'On Glory.'",
                              "symbolism": "The Crown represents ultimate knowledge corrupted by the lust for power. Francisco's willingness to act alone symbolizes how pride separates us from our support systems. The bargain itself represents the first step toward his eventual humbling.",
