        "2nd_trilogy": {
          "trilogy_title": "The Wheel of Realms",
          "trilogy_description": "This middle trilogy escalates the cosmic chess match ignited in The Trionfi Genesis. As Francisco, La Signora, and their fractured allies pursue control over the Trionfi and the fates they represent, the very structure of time becomes unstable. Alternate versions of key characters emerge, each with competing agendas. The Wheel of Realms begins to spin—pulling realities into orbit around warring philosophies of fate, memory, and authorship. What began as rebellion becomes recursion. Victory may lie not in seizing power, but in surviving the infinite variations of the self.",
          "trilogy_purpose": "This trilogy explores the consequences of disrupting divine order. With the Trionfi scattered and unstable, each realm now exerts its own gravitational pull on the multiverse. The purpose is to deepen character arcs by confronting them with alternate selves, moral ambiguity, and existential echoes. Themes of recursion, sovereignty, and divergence are introduced. New forces emerge claiming authorship over fate. As timelines collapse and realms fracture, the true cost of liberation comes into focus—forcing each character to question who they are, what they fight for, and whether any version of history can be trusted.",
          "trilogy_tagline": "Rebellion isn’t just against the gods—it’s against the very story they wrote you into.",
          "trilogy_logline": "As timelines unravel and divine hierarchies retaliate, a rebellious circle of mortals must forge impossible alliances and master the fractured Trionfi to ignite a war—not just against gods, but against fate itself.",
          "trilogy_themes": [
            "The Corruption of Power: What happens when liberators become architects of their own systems?",
            "Identity Collapse Across Timelines: Who are you when your alternate selves make opposing choices?",
            "The Cost of Enlightenment: Seeing too much may burn away what it means to be human.",
            "Rebellion as Evolution: Recursion becomes necessary—until the wheel is broken.",
            "Temporal Sovereignty: Realms fight not for dominance, but for the right to define what is real."
          ],
          "narrative_arc": {
            "beginning": "The Trionfi scatter across divergent realms, each bending time and reality to their unique archetypal forces.",
            "middle": "Francisco and La Signora struggle to reclaim agency as alternate selves and hidden players rewrite their stories.",
            "end": "The Wheel of Realms reaches a breaking point, threatening to erase the concept of linear cause-and-effect—and unravel memory itself."
          },
          "tone": [
            "Philosophical",
            "Psychological",
            "Mythic",
            "Chaotic",
            "Haunting"
          ],
          "main_locales": [
            "The Mirrorfold",
            "The Broken Basilica",
            "The Clockwork Sea",
            "The Ruins of Mechanikos",
            "Temporal Refuge Zones"
          ],
          "worldbuilding_milestones": [
            "The Wheel of Realms is introduced as a temporal meta-structure.",
            "The Trionfi fracture into sub-arcana, each tied to divergent realms.",
            "Time is no longer linear—events are experienced recursively or simultaneously.",
            "New players emerge from collapsed or hidden timelines claiming authorship rights."
          ],
          "pivotal_events": [
            "Francisco confronts an alternate version of himself that never chose to write.",
            "La Signora absorbs a corrupted Trionfi archetype and begins to rewrite others’ memories.",
            "The Zanetti Train derails, birthing a paradox loop in Pangaea.",
            "A divine tribunal attempts to rewrite Francisco’s soul into myth—forcing him to sever himself from history."
          ],
          "emotional_arc": "From conviction to disorientation, fragmentation, and the agonizing reassembly of purpose and selfhood.",
          "core_questions": [
            "What happens when multiple versions of 'you' seek different outcomes?",
            "If memory can be rewritten, is any choice truly yours?",
            "Can freedom endure when no truth is fixed?",
            "What defines identity when time is recursive?",
            "Is revolution still righteous when it begins to mirror the gods it sought to destroy?"
          ],
          "trilogy_consequence": "The structure of time collapses into a spinning wheel of coexisting realms. Characters fracture, merge, or vanish into versions of themselves. What survives this cycle will inherit not just power—but the responsibility to define what remains real.",
